ions.","label":"","list":false,"name":"pollerIntervalInMilliSeconds","properties":null,"required":false,"type":"integer","values":null,"valuesSource":"definition"},{"addressByName":false,"allowUserProperties":false,"allowUserValue":true,"defaultValue":20,"description":"Uses data partitions to read data from the database. This setting has the largest impact on overall indexing time. As such, SAS recommends changing this setting if you need to improve performance. This should be set high, but not so high that it causes memory consumption or resource issues. Consider whether you need extra performance to handle your workloads before increasing this setting. A suggested tuning exercise when resourcing issues are hit is to reduce this setting to 5 or 10 and then gradually increase it until you strike a balance between stability and performance.Note: Each instance of the Indexer contains its own set of workers. As a result, you might typically choose to increase the number of Indexer instances rather than increase the worker count.","label":"","list":false,"name":"workers.dataIndexer.numberOfWorkers","properties":null,"required":false,"type":"integer","values":null,"valuesSource":"definition"},{"addressByName":false,"allowUserProperties":false,"allowUserValue":true,"defaultValue":10,"description":"Removes data partition descriptors from the queue. This worker has a very simple job and leaving the default setting is recommended.","label":"","list":false,"name":"workers.dataIndexerLauncher.numberOfWorkers","properties":null,"required":false,"type":"integer","values":null,"valuesSource":"definition"},{"addressByName":false,"allowUserProperties":false,"allowUserValue":true,"defaultValue":10,"description":"Adds data partition descriptors to the queue. Each Data Partitioner worker can produce several partitions. SAS recommends setting the number of workers larger than that configured for Data Partitioner.","label":"","list":false,"name":"workers.dataPartitionInitializer.numberOfWorkers","properties":null,"required":false,"type":"integer","values":null,"valuesSource":"definition"},{"addressByName":false,"allowUserProperties":false,"allowUserValue":true,"defaultValue":10,"description":"Partitions the data for each entity, relationship, or transaction type in the indexing job. Each worker partitions one object type. Setting this to the number of object types is recommended, assuming there are enough database connections available and the Indexer has enough allocated memory.","label":"","list":false,"name":"workers.dataPartitioner.numberOfWorkers","properties":null,"required":false,"type":"integer","values":null,"valuesSource":"definition"},{"addressByName":false,"allowUserProperties":false,"allowUserValue":true,"defaultValue":10,"description":"Starts indexing jobs. This can be used to control the number of jobs that can be launched at the same time. Note that jobs can’t run simultaneously, they are queued. This setting has little impact for most scenarios, leaving the default setting is thus recommended.","label":"","list":false,"name":"workers.jobLauncher.numberOfWorkers","properties":null,"required":false,"type":"integer","values":null,"valuesSource":"definition"},{"addressByName":false,"allowUserProperties":false,"allowUserValue":true,"defaultValue":50,"description":"This worker loads associated data for objects such as child entities, attachments, insights, and comments. Increasing this setting allows the Indexer to process more of this data simultaneously, at the cost of greater memory consumption. On an environment with large amounts of associated data, for example, a high number of child objects per parent object, you might need to reduce this setting from the default to reduce memory consumption.","label":"","list":false,"name":"workers.objectEnricher.numberOfWorkers","properties":null,"required":false,"type":"integer","values":null,"valuesSource":"definition"},{"addressByName":false,"allowUserProperties":false,"allowUserValue":true,